  "body": "We currently don’t allow `input` elements in the Color state to have the `pattern` and `placeholder` attributes.\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#color-state-(type=color)\r\n\r\nHowever, at https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/input/color#Value the relevant MDN article says:\r\n\r\n> Any web browser that doesn't support an <code>input</code> of type <code>color</code> will naturally fall back to the type <code>text</code>. Because users can write colors in many different ways (such as <code>rgb(255, 255, 0)</code>), one way to work around this is to set a <code>pattern</code> attribute equal to either <code>#[0-9a-f]{6}</code> or <code>#[0-9A-Fa-f]{6}</code> (preferably the latter because some users may not expect a different result with just a letter-case change) and set a <code>placeholder</code> attribute equal to a color representing the expected format of the user's input, such as <code>#ff0000</code>.\r\n\r\nOn the face of it, that MDN guidance doesn’t seem bad, and it looks like a reasonable use case for allowing `pattern` and `placeholder` for `input` elements in the Color state. And if so, we should change the spec to allow it.\r\n\r\nBut if that MDN language is instead not actually good guidance, then I can update the article to change that language.",
